# SYNOPSIS

```text
/lib/theme/NAME
```

# DESCRIPTION

A theme is one text file in ndb(6)'s format: entries of attribute=value pairs, an entry beginning at the left margin and going on over the indented lines after it.
A value with a space in it is quoted, as `family="IBM Plex Sans"`; a quoted value has no escapes, so it can't hold a quote.
`#` begins a comment wherever it stands, even straight after `=`, so colours are written `0x0a0a12`, not `#0a0a12`.

A file is records.
A record begins at a head pair and takes the pairs after it, up to the next head; the heads are `theme`, `role`, `typeface`, `part`, `knob`, `parts`, `textscale` and `variant`.
A record that has an attribute its kind doesn't take is an error, named with its file and line.
Roles, parts and knobs are named in dotted words of lower-case letters, digits and hyphens: `ink.on-select`, `window.tab.focused`.

```text
# Neon Tab, cut down.
theme=neon-tab title="Neon Tab" inherits=base version=1
	author="todhchai's storyboard, ported" licence=BSD-3-Clause
role=desk color=0x0a0a12
role=chrome color=0x12121e
role=neon.cyan color=0x00e5ff
role=panel color=chrome
typeface=body family="IBM Plex Sans" weight=400
typeface=ui family="Barlow Condensed" weight=500
part=button stroke=control ink=ink border=1
	font=ui weight=600 size=12.8 tracking=0.08 case=upper
	padding=5.76,11.52,5.76,11.52
part=button.primary stroke=neon.cyan ink=neon.cyan
part=code.highlight fill=neon.cyan@14
knob=accent type=role default=neon.cyan suggest=neon.cyan,neon.amber
	label="Accent colour"
part=window.body.focused stroke=$accent glow=5.76 glowcolor=neon.glow
variant=high-contrast title="Neon Tab, high contrast"
	role=desk color=0x000000
	role=chrome color=0x000000
	textscale=1.25
```

The records:

: `theme=NAME`
  The file's first record: its name, which is its file's name, and `inherits=` the theme beneath it (the base if none).
  `title=`, `author=`, `licence=` and `version=` describe it.
: `role=NAME color=COLOUR`
  A colour by its role.
  Every theme resolves the required roles, its own or inherited: the surfaces `desk`, `chrome`, `chrome.behind`, `well`, `menu` and `panel`; the edges `outline`, `bevel.light`, `bevel.dark` and `control`; the ink `ink`, `ink.dim`, `ink.disabled`, `ink.on-select` and `ink.on-focus`; the meanings `focus`, `selection`, `live`, `alert`, `warning` and `realtime`; `lcd` and `lcd.ink`; the lamps `lamp.local`, `lamp.agent`, `lamp.egress` and `lamp.swarm`; and the terminal's `term.0` to `term.15`.
  A theme may add roles of its own (`neon.cyan`) for its parts; apps ask only for the required ones.
: `typeface=ROLE family=NAME`
  The face for one of the four font roles, `display`, `body`, `mono` and `ui`, with `weight=` and `width=` (a percentage of normal).
  A program finds it among the faces it has by its family's name and the nearest width, with every weight of that width (uicore(2)).
: `part=NAME`
  A class's style.
  A part takes its fields from its nearest defined dot-parent (`button` for `button.primary`), or from `default`, and then its own: `fill`, `stroke`, `ink` and `glowcolor`, colours; `border`, `radius` and `glow`, lengths; `cuts`, four lengths cutting the corners at 45° (top left, top right, bottom right, bottom left); `padding`, one length or four (top, right, bottom, left); `font`, a font role; `weight`; `size`, in pixels an em; `lineheight`, a multiple of the size; `tracking`, in ems; and `case=upper` or `case=as-is`.
  Materials over the fill: `gradient=FROM,TO` with `angle=` in degrees; `bevel=raised`, `inset` or `groove` with `bevelwidth=`; `pattern=stripes`, `checks` or `dots` with `patterncolors=A,B` and `pitch=`; and `shadow=COLOUR` with `shadowoffset=X,Y` and `blur=`.
  `none` takes one away.
  A pressed class, `NAME.held`, that the theme doesn't define is drawn as NAME with its ink at 20% inside, its edge and label in its ink.
: `knob=NAME type=TYPE default=VALUE`
  A parameter people may turn, referred to as `$NAME` wherever a colour or length goes.
  A `role` knob's value is a colour, and `suggest=` lists the ones to offer; a `choice` knob's is one of `choices=`; a `length` or `scale` knob's is a number from `min=` to `max=`.
  `label=` is what the Appearance panel shows.
  `knob=NAME value=VALUE`, in a layer or a theme above, turns it.
: `parts=FAMILY from=THEME`
  Takes a family of parts, `window.*` (the name and everything under it), a name, or `*`, from another theme, in place of this theme's own.
  The borrowed parts are drawn in this theme's roles; a role they name that this theme lacks takes the lender's.
: `textscale=N`
  Makes all type N times larger.
: `variant=NAME`
  Makes the rest of its entry that variant's: records that apply only when the variant is chosen, over the whole chain.
  `title=` names it.

A colour is `0xrrggbb` or `0xrrggbbaa` in sRGB, `none`, a role, a role at a percentage of its alpha (`neon.cyan@14`), or a knob, at an alpha too (`$accent@20`).
A role may name another role, which is resolved last, so a variant that changes `chrome` changes the `panel` that names it.
Lengths are logical pixels.

Colours are chosen in sRGB, and everything drawn in them is blended in linear light (light(2)).
Text's weight is the base theme's knob `text.contrast`, a scale from 0 to 1, 0.6 unless a theme or a layer turns it: at 0 a glyph's coverage is blended as it is, and higher settings draw ink darker than its ground heavier and lighter ink thinner.
At 0.6 the base theme's body text has the weight it had when the bytes were mixed.

## Layers

What is drawn is a stack, each layer a file of this format naming only what it changes.
From the bottom: the system's base; the theme's chain, through `inherits`; its variant; the user's layer; the workspace's; the output's; and an app rule's.
High contrast is a switch over them all: the chain's `high-contrast` variant (the base's, if the theme has none), with glow off.
A layer has no `theme=` record.
A user's layer that turns a knob and changes the mono face:

```text
knob=accent value=neon.amber
typeface=mono family="IBM Plex Mono" weight=500
```

A layer that fails to parse or resolve is refused with its file and line, and the stack beneath it stands.

## The check

`theme check` (vx-theme(1) on the dev machine) resolves a theme in each of its variants, and again with each knob at its extremes (each suggestion or choice, a length's bounds) and the others at their defaults, and fails it if:

- a required role doesn't resolve;
- `ink` or `ink.dim` is under 4.5:1 against a surface but the desk, or `ink.on-select` on `selection`, `ink.on-focus` on `focus`, or `lcd.ink` on `lcd`;
- `control`, a meaning or a lamp is under 3:1 against a surface (the desk aside) or, for a lamp, `chrome`;
- two lamps are the same colour;
- a part with an opaque fill has ink under 4.5:1 on it (a part whose ink is its fill, a rule, draws no text and is passed).

Text on the desk is the `desktop` part's, and is judged as a part: a dark desk beneath light windows, as Platinum Indigo's, writes its labels in an ink of their own.

Contrast is WCAG 2.2's.

No theme reaches the trusted prompt: radharc(4) draws it in colours of its own, in every theme.

# FILES

: `/lib/theme/NAME`
  The installed themes, one file each, in the desktop's images; radharc(4) reads them and serves the theme in force at `/svc/windows/theme`.
: `ui/theme/themes/`
  The shipped themes, in the tree: Platinum Indigo, the default, and Neon Tab.

